WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.960
There's nothing wrong with a grim ending if
it fits the movie, but it's all too easy to

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.759
go too far down that dark rabbit hole.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:11.880
Whether they're comedies, horror movies, or
summer blockbusters, these movies all started

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.560
out as total downers… until the filmmakers
saw the light and decided not to send their

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.039
audiences out of the theater with a serious
case of the blues.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:21.989
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:23.130
Rocky

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.619
There's no doubt that the Rocky movies are
some of the most uplifting films ever made.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:32.439
They're all about finding love, chasing your
dreams, and proving you're someone special.

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:37.600
On top of that, this franchise is absolutely
jam-packed with inspirational montages.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.899
But for a series that's so positive, Sylvester
Stallone initially planned on taking these

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:44.250
films in some pretty dark directions.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:49.239
According to Entertainment Weekly, Stallone's
original ending was downright depressing.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:53.420
Not only does Rocky lose, but in this version,
he actually throws the fight and then opens

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.649
a pet store.

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:59.199
Nock the same punch as the Italian Stallion
going toe-to-toe with the champ for 15 straight

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:00.600
rounds.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.059
Get Out

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:06.380
The ending of Jordan Peele's Get Out is one
of the greatest fake-outs in horror movie

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.380
history.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.660
After going full Rambo on his white kidnappers,
Chris finds himself in a rather tricky situation.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.619
Kneeling over the body of his evil ex-girlfriend,
Chris suddenly sees a cop car coming down

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:18.619
the road.

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:22.990
Fortunately, it's just Chris's TSA buddy,
Rod, come to save the day.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.619
Talking to Buzzfeed's Another Round podcast,
Peele admitted that originally, a real cop

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:31.780
was supposed to roll up and mistake Chris
for a criminal, giving the film a more realistic

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.890
and darker ending.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.009
He explained:

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:39.759
"It was very clear that, like, the ending
needed to transform into something that gives

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:46.329
us a hero, that gives us an escape, gives
us a positive feeling when we leave the movie."

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:50.719
Peele himself liked the theatrical ending
much better than the darker original one,

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:51.719
adding:

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:56.579
"There's nothing more satisfying than seeing
the audience go crazy when Rod shows up."

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:57.729
The Silence of the Lambs

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:03.829
Sophisticated, intelligent, and always hungry,
Hannibal Lecter sent a chill through our collective

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:06.510
spine at the end of The Silence of the Lambs.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.750
After escaping the courthouse, the psycho
psychiatrist makes his way to the tropics,

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:14.890
where he gives Agent Starling a phone call
before stalking the doctor who tormented him

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:15.890
for so many years.

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:23.350
"I do wish we could chat longer, but I'm having
an old friend for dinner."

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:28.760
It's a macabre way to end a film, but Ted
Tally's screenplay originally had a far darker

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.430
scene planned for the ending.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.750
This time, Lecter is calling from Chilton's
office.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.800
After saying goodbye to Agent Starling, he
puts down the phone and turns to Dr. Chilton…who's

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.280
tied up in a chair.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.180
Lecter then walks toward him with the knife
and asks, "Shall we begin?"

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.700
As director Jonathan Demme explained to Deadline:

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.430
"It was too horrifying a way to close the
proceedings…

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.940
I thought this story deserved more to it than
the crude, trussed-up Dr. Chilton about to

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.860
be carved by Dr. Lecter."

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.860
So Tally rewrote the scene and put Hannibal
in the Caribbean so audiences could see him

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.840
"out in the world."

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.420
Groundhog Day

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.950
One of the greatest comedies ever made, Groundhog
Day was written by Danny Rubin and directed

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.910
by Harold Ramis—but the two filmmakers had
very different ideas about the ending.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:17.790
In the movie, weatherman Phil Connors eventually
becomes a good person, breaks the curse that

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:22.570
makes him keep reliving February 2nd, and
wakes up the next day, finally free from his

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:23.950
Groundhog Day hell.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.260
But in Rubin's script, Phil then discovers
that Rita is trapped in a time loop of her

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.810
own, stuck in February 3rd for the foreseeable
future.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.590
As The New Yorker put it, this is a pretty
"bleak" ending, and we've got to say, Ramis

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.950
definitely made the right choice by altering
the screenplay.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.120
Rise of the Planet of the Apes

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.620
When it comes to dark endings, it's really
hard to beat the original Planet of the Apes

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:47.300
series.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.400
"Oh my God..."

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.420
But the recent reboots have definitely tried.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:03.090
In 2011's Rise of the Planet of the Apes,
Caesar and Will Rodman part ways in the woods

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.730
after the climactic Golden Gate Bridge battle.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:10.920
But on the page, things take a much more tragic
turn: According to producer Dylan Clark, the

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.830
guy who runs the primate prison follows Caesar
into the woods and shoots Rodman.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.570
But this ending didn't sit well with Clark,
who said:

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.819
"The scene, dramatically, was idiotic.

00:04:20.819 --> 00:04:22.970
And worse, it just made you feel bad.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.030
It was just like, 'Oh my God, this is the
worst movie ever."

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:28.460
Split

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.210
The theatrical release of Split doesn't have
the most upbeat ending, but as it turns out,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.940
the movie could've gone in an even darker
direction.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.469
Writer-director M. Night Shyamalan originally
intended to wrap things up with Kevin sitting

00:04:40.469 --> 00:04:44.550
outside a school, waiting for all the children
to come running outside.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.919
That's when the credits would roll, strongly
implying the Beast was about to feast.

00:04:48.919 --> 00:04:52.110
However, Shyamalan said this was way too dark:

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.729
"It made [the end] kind of feel one note for
me about what [the Beast's] intentions were.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.159
I just didn't want the Beast's motivations
to be reduced to just killing, that kind of

00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:02.159
thing.

00:05:02.159 --> 00:05:04.509
So that's why I wrote the ending you see in
the movie."

00:05:04.509 --> 00:05:05.509
Thanks for watching!

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:08.050
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.179
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

